paragraph.math:
$$\begin{matrix}
       & {\left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      0 & {- 1} \\
      1 & 0
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      {- i}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      i \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack = i\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      {- i}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack} \\
       & {\left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      0 & {- 1} \\
      1 & 0
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      i
      \end{array} \right\rbrack = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- i} \\
      1
      \end{array} \right\rbrack = - i\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      i
      \end{array} \right\rbrack}
      \end{matrix}$$

example.paragraph:
Thus $i$ and $- i$ are eigenvalues, with $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      {- i}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ and $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      i
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ as corresponding eigenvectors. (A method for finding complex eigenvectors is discussed in Example 2.)

If $\textbf{x} = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
      {3 - i} \\
      i \\
      {2 + 5i}
      \end{bmatrix}~ = ~\begin{bmatrix}
      3 \\
      0 \\
      2
      \end{bmatrix}~ + ~i\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 1} \\
      1 \\
      5
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$, then

paragraph.math:
$$\begin{array}{r}
      {A = PCP^{- 1},\quad\text{where}\quad P = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      {\text{Re~}\textbf{v}} & {\text{Im~}\textbf{v}}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack\quad\text{and}\quad C = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      a & {- b} \\
      b & a
      \end{array} \right\rbrack}
      \end{array}$$

example.paragraph:
The matrix $A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rrc}
      .8 & {{- \text{.}}6} & 0 \\
      .6 & {\text{.}8} & 0 \\
      0 & 0 & {1.07}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ has eigenvalues $.8 \pm .6i$ and $1.07$. Any vector $\textbf{w}_{0}$ in the $x_{1}x_{2}\text{-plane}$ (with third coordinate $0$) is rotated by $A$ into another point in the plane. Any vector $\textbf{x}_{0}$ not in the plane has its $x_{3}\text{-coordinate}$ multiplied by $1.07$. The iterates of the points $\textbf{w}_{0} = (2,0,0)$ and $\textbf{x}_{0} = (2,0,1)$ under multiplication by $A$ are shown in Figure 5.

statement.paragraph:
In Example 2, solve the first equation in (2) for $x_{2}$ in terms of $x_{1}$, and from that produce the eigenvector $\textbf{y} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      2 \\
      {- 1 + 2i}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ for the matrix $A$. Show that this $\textbf{y}$ is a (complex) multiple of the vector $\textbf{v}_{1}$ used in Example 2.
